ion\n\nThe starting point of the present paper is the following result of G´acs, H´eger, Nagy and\n\nP´alv¨olgyi.\n\nTheorem 1.1.[8] Let {a 1, a 2,...,a p}be a multiset in the ﬁnite ﬁeld GF (p),p a prime.\n\nThen after a suitable permutation of the indices, either P i ia i= 0, or a 1=a 2=··· =\n\na p−2=a,a p−1=a+b,a p=a−b for ﬁeld elements a and b,b 6= 0.\n\nA similar result using a slightly diﬀerent terminology was obtained by Vinatier [12]\n\nunder the extra assumption that a 1,...,a p, when considered as nonnegative integers,\n\nsatisfy a 1+···+a p=p.\n\nThe former result can be extended to arbitrary ﬁnite ﬁelds in the following sense.\n\nTheorem 1.2.[8] Let {a 1, a 2,...,a q}be a multiset in the ﬁnite ﬁeld GF (q). There are\n\nno distinct ﬁeld elements b 1, b 2,...,b q such that P i a i b i= 0 if and only if after a suitable\n\npermutation of the indices, a 1=a 2=··· =a q−2=a,a q−1=a+b,a q=a−b for some\n\nﬁeld elements a and b,b 6= 0.\n\nIn the background of these results is Alon’s Combinatorial Nullstellensatz [1], with\n\nhaving applications in ﬁnite geometry and about range of polynomials over ﬁnite ﬁeld.\n\nOur aim is to ﬁnd a diﬀerent kind of generalization, more combinatorial in nature,\n\nwhich use only the group structure. First we extend the result to cyclic groups of odd\n\norder.\n\nTheorem 1.3.Let {a 1, a 2,...,a m}be a multiset in an Abelian group Z m,m is odd. Then\n\nafter a suitable permutation of the indices, either P i ia i= 0, or a 1=a 2=··· =a m−2=a,\n\na m−1=a+b,a m=a−b for elements a and b,(b, m) = 1.\n\nThe situation is somewhat diﬀerent if the order of the group is even. In this case we\n\nhave to deal with two types of exceptional structures. To describe them, denote by exp(m)\n\nthe greatest exponent t of 2 for which m is divisible by 2 t. The following statements are\n\neasy to check.\n\nProposition 1.4.Let m be an even number. If a multiset M={a 1, a 2,...,a m}of\n\nZ m consists of elements having the same odd residue c(mod 2 exp(m)), then M has no\n\npermutation for which P i ia i= 0 holds. If M={a, a, . . . , a +b, a −b}(mod m), where\n\na is even and (b, m) = 1 holds, then M has no permutation for which P i ia i= 0 holds.\n\nDate: 29 December 2009.\n\nThis research was supported by Hungarian National Scientiﬁc Research Funds (OTKA) grant 81310.\n\n1\n\n2 ZOLT ´\n\nAN L. NAGY\n\nWe call them homogeneous and inhomogeneous exceptional multisets.\n\nTheorem 1.5.Suppose M={a 1, a 2,...,a m}is a multiset in an Abelian group Z m,\n\nm is even. Let us write m as m= 2 k Q, where Q is odd. If M is not a homogeneous\n\nor inhomogeneous exceptional multiset, then after a suitable permutation of the indices\n\nP i ia i= 0 holds.\n\nThese investigations were partly motivated by a recent work of Britnell and Wildon [4],\nIn Section 5 we present\n\nthe proof of Theorem 1.5, the even order case. Finally we discuss some open problems.\n\n2. Preliminaries\n\nWe start with the reformulation of the main question.\n\nDeﬁnition 2.1.Let M={a 1,...,a m}be a multiset in Z m. A permutational sum of the\n\nelements of M is any sum of the form P m\n\ni=1 ia π(i), for some permutation π∈S m.\n\nBy this, the question in center of attention is to determine the multisets for which the\n\npermutational sums do not admit the value zero.\n\nThe following three simple observations will be used frequently throughout the paper.\n\nProposition 2.2.Assume that m is odd. If no permutational sum of M admits the value\n\n0, then the same holds for any translation M+c of M, and also for any multiple cM in\n\ncase (c, m) = 1.\n\nProposition 2.3.If a permutational sum of M admits a value w, then it also admits\n\nthe value kw for every integer k with (m, k) = 1. As a consequence, if (m, w) = 1, then\n\nthe permutational sum takes at least ϕ(m)diﬀerent values.\n\nProposition 2.4.Assume that m is odd, and M has the exceptional structure, i.e.\n\nM={a, . . . , a, a +b, a −b}where (b, m) = 1. Then the permutational sum of M admit\n\nall elements of Z m except zero.\n\nThe following proposition is a simple consequence of the pigeonhole principle.\n\nProposition 2.5.If we have two sets in Z m, and the sum of their cardinalities is greater\n\n\na i+p k−3. We similarly ﬁnish the proof, except all the residues are the same again. Re-\n\npeating this argument for the raised exponent, we get ﬁnally that every multiset can be\n\nordered well except the one containing t m −2 times, t+B and t−B, one of each, with\n\nthe condition (B, p) = 1 and t an arbitrary element of Z m. That way we see that M itself\n\nhas the exceptional structure for which no zero permutational sum exists.\n\n4. Proof of Theorem 1.3, arbitrary odd order case\n\nIn this section, we extend the proof to a more general form: to the case of Z m where\n\nm is odd. Take the prime factorization of the order, let p be a prime divisor of m and\n\nsuppose its highest power that divides m is p k. Let us denote by Q the remaining term,\n\nso m=p k Q.\n\nApplying the procedure of the First and Second step in Section 2, we get that the\n\npermutational sum Φ of form Φ = R+p k−1 Q P p−1\n\ni=0 iS i+1 have the following property:\n\nPERMUTATIONS OVER CYCLIC GROUPS 7\n\np k−1 Q|R, and hence p k−1 Q|Φ for a well chosen permutation, which is got from the\n\ninitial order by applying a suitable block preserving permutation. If no such initial order\n\nexists, then the multiset must have a special structure, for which we prove the theorem\n\nin Lemma 4.2 in the end of the section.\n\nIn the next step, we can rewrite the formula: m|Φ = R+p k−1 Q P p−1\n\ni=0 iS i+1 ⇔\n\np|Φ′=R′+P p−1\n\ni=0 iS i+1, where R′=m\n\np R.\n\nOur aim is to (re)order the blocks if possible so that p|Φ′holds.\n\nWe distinguish two cases according to whether R′is divisible by p or not.\n\n4.1. R′is not divisible by p.\n\nNow we have a multiset consisting of p elements: {S 1, S 2,...,S p}, and we need a per-\n\nmutation for which its permutational sum is equal to −R′(mod p).\n\nLike in Subsection 3.1, such a permutation exists according to the propositions, except\n\nwhen every S i is equal (mod p). We use the braid trick: we look at (a i, a i+Qp k−1) pairs for\n\nevery i, and follow the above proof to conclude that in this case, there exists a convenient\n\n\npermutation that ensures Q|Φ. Clearly, the indices of the even elements are divisible by\n\nQ. After this, reorder the blocks so that the ﬁrst and second block become the m\n\n2 th and\n\nm th, that is, a m\n\n2=q 1 and a m=q 2. Hence, Q|Φ and 2 k−1|Φ too, via equality (*). If\n\n2 k|Φ not holds, then an element c∗must exist that we can exchange with an element c,\n\n(similarly to the case Q= 1) to ﬁnish the proof. \n\nReferences\n\n[1] N. Alon, Combinatorial Nullstellensatz, Combin. Prob. Comp. 8(1999), 7-29.\n\n[2] N. Alon, Additive Latin transversals. Israel J. Math. 117 (2000), 125-130.\n\n[3] B. Arsovski, The proof of Snevily’s conjecture, Israel J. Math 182 (2011), 505-508.\n\n14 ZOLT ´\n\nAN L. NAGY\n\n[4] J.R. Britnell, M. Wildon, On types and classes of commuting matrices over ﬁnite ﬁelds,\n\nhttp://arxiv.org/pdf/1001.0811v1.\n\n[5] S. Dasgupta, Gy. K´\n\narolyi, O. Serra, B. Szegedy, Transversals of additive Latin squares. Israel\n\nJ. Math. 126 (2001), 17-28.\n\n[6] P. Erd˝\n\nos, A. Ginzburg, A. Ziv, Theorem in additive number theory, Bull. Research Council,\n\nView\n\nShow abstract\n\nOn types and classes of commuting matrices over finite fields\n\nArticle\n\n  * Jan 2010\n\n  * John R. Britnell\n  * Mark Wildon\n\nThis paper addresses various questions about pairs of similarity classes of matrices that contain commuting elements. In the case of matrices over finite fields, we show that the problem of determining such pairs reduces to a question about nilpotent classes; this reduction makes use of class types in the sense of Steinberg and Green. We investigate the set of scalars that arise as determinants of elements of the centralizer algebra of a matrix, providing a complete description of this set in terms of the class type of the matrix. Several results are established concerning the commuting of nilpotent classes. Classes that are represented in the centralizer of every nilpotent matrix are classified; this result holds over any field. Nilpotent classes are parametrized by partitions; we find pairs of partitions whose corresponding nilpotent classes commute over some finite fields, but not over others. We conclude by classifying all pairs of classes, parametrized by two-part partitions, that commute. Our results on nilpotent classes complement work of Oblak and of Košir and Oblak.\n\nView\n\nShow abstract\n\nShow more\n--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nBibTeX bibliography texbook2.bib (https://ftp.math.utah.edu/pub/tex/bib/texbook2.html)\nciteturn1search2 [wordlim: 200] Published: 2.5 years ago; Crawled: 2.5 years ago;                      3.2 Floor/Ceiling Applications / 70 \\\\\n      remark-2 =     \"See translations\n                     \\cite{Knuth:1990:JTS,Knuth:1991:JTS,Knuth:1992:MD,Knuth:1992:xx,Knuth:1993:KS,Knuth:199x:xxc}.\",\n      shorttableofcontents = \"1: Recurrent Problems / 1 \\\\\n                     2: Sums / 21 \\\\\n                     3: Integer Functions / 67 \\\\\n                     4: Number Theory / 102 \\\\\n                     5: Binomial Coefficients / 153 \\\\\n                     6: Special Numbers / 243 \\\\\n                     7: Generating Functions / 306 \\\\\n                     8: Discrete Probability / 367 \\\\\n                     9: Asymptotics / 425 \\\\\n                     A: Answers to Exercises / 483 \\\\\n                     B: Bibliography / 578 \\\\\n                     C: Credits for Exercises / 601 \\\\\n                     Index / 606 \\\\\n                     List of Tables / 624\",\n      tableofcontents = \"1: Recurrent Problems / 1 \\\\\n                     1.1 The Tower of Hanoi / 1 \\\\\n                     1.